From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] Migrate 0388-frizti-v2-04-shader-translation-without-full-scans.patch

Source: 0388-frizti-v2-04-shader-translation-without-full-scans.patch
shader: find visited blocks and sibling order without full list scans (off by default)

Core patch. Paths are the submodule's. Applies on core16 alone.

Source: Frizti/Lichii f8f29b5769 (Lichii #71, by Zortos with capy-ai), the shader part only.

EDEN_NX2_SHADER_FAST_SCAN=1. CFG::InspectVisitedBlocks scanned every block of the function for each
label, and AreOrdered walked every statement after the right sibling, so both grew with the square
of the program size. On PC one 253k-word Kirby compute shader took 1.6 s, a third in each. Blocks
never overlap, so an ordered lookup finds the one that holds pc, and walking from both siblings at
once costs only the shorter walk. Same answers, less time.

Why here: pipelines translate on the GPU thread, and each one stalls it; Zen 2 pays more per scan.
Ocarina's share is unknown: its programs are smaller than that Kirby shader.

Changed from the source: off by default, there always on. Old code kept for the switch. Includes
<cstdlib> and <iterator>, which the new lines use.

Runs on the PS5: same core path.
Hang: none. AreOrdered stops at the list end either way, a finite walk.
Pool memory: none.
Checked: applies on core16. PS5-target syntax compile of both files, no errors. Same logic against the
old scans: AreOrdered 38,024 pairs, block lookup 1.2 million queries, 0 differences. These tests ran
the logic on plain containers, not boost's intrusive ones. Not run on the console.

Co-authored-by: Zortos <zortosdev@proton.me>
Co-authored-by: capy-ai[bot] <230910855+capy-ai[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>

